# Advisories that scripts/advisories.py accepts: id | package | reason | date reviewed (yyyy-mm-dd)
# Add a line only after reading the advisory and checking that our code does not reach it. Say why.
RUSTSEC-2024-0388 | derivative | Unmaintained. Only pingora-core 0.9.1 uses it, as a derive macro, so it runs at build time and no derivative code is linked into the agent. pingora 0.9.1 is the latest release and still depends on it. Upstream: https://github.com/cloudflare/pingora/issues/889 | 2026-11-16
RUSTSEC-2024-0449 | unsafe-libyaml | Unmaintained. Linked through serde_yaml in pingora-core 0.9.1, which reads pingora's YAML server config. Our code never calls that (no ServerConf or Opt) and parses no YAML. pingora 0.9.1 is the latest release. Upstream: https://github.com/cloudflare/pingora/issues/1058, replacement in review: https://github.com/cloudflare/pingora/pull/1117 | 2026-11-16
RUSTSEC-2025-0134 | rustls-pemfile | Unmaintained, PEM parsing only. Linked through pingora-rustls 0.9.1 and its rustls-native-certs 0.7, for system roots and client certificates; our code does not call it. pingora 0.9.1 is the latest release. Upstream: https://github.com/cloudflare/pingora/pull/1011 | 2026-11-16
RUSTSEC-2026-0543 | chrono | Unmaintained, no fixed version. Linked through pingora-core 0.9.1 with default features off (only "alloc"), in one place: protocols/http/date.rs formats the clock of the server into the Date response header. No input from the network reaches it, and our code does not use chrono. pingora 0.9.1 is the latest release and still depends on it. Advisory: https://osv.dev/RUSTSEC-2026-0543 | 2026-11-17
